What is the historical context of Frances Vane, Marchioness Of Londonderry's life, specifically her marital status and relationships?
Frances Vane, Marchioness of Londonderry, was married to Charles William Stewart, 1st Baron Stewart, who later changed his surname to hers and became Charles William Vane. She became the Marchioness of Londonderry in 1822 when her husband succeeded his half-brother as the 3rd Marquess of Londonderry. 

She was a patron of Benjamin Disraeli and sought to promote the political career of her eldest son, George Vane-Tempest. Her husband died in 1854, and she commissioned an equestrian statue of him, which was unveiled in 1861.

Through her daughter, Lady Frances Vane, wife of John Churchill, 7th Duke of Marlborough, she is the great-grandmother of Sir Winston Churchill.
