What is the historical context of Setepenre (Princess) and her family?
Setepenre (Princess) was a member of the 18th dynasty of ancient Egypt, specifically the family of Pharaoh Akhenaten and his chief queen Nefertiti. She was one of six daughters, born around the 9th to 11th year of Akhenaten's reign, and was the sixth and last daughter of the royal couple. The family lived in the city of Akhetaten, and their lives were documented through various frescoes and tombs, including the King's House in Amarna and the Royal Tomb of Akhenaten.

The historical context of Setepenre's family is marked by the reign of Akhenaten, who introduced significant changes to Egyptian society and culture, including the worship of a single deity, Aten. The family's life was also affected by a possible plague that swept across Egypt during Akhenaten's 12th to 15th regnal years, which led to the deaths of several members of the royal family, including Queen Mother Tiye, Queen Nefertiti, and two of the youngest princesses, Neferneferure and Setepenre.

The family's relationships and interactions are also documented through various depictions, such as the fresco in the King's House in Amarna, where Setepenre is shown sitting on her mother's lap, and the tomb of Meryre II, where the royal family is shown receiving foreign tributes. These depictions provide a glimpse into the lives of the royal family and their relationships with each other and with the outside world.
